                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "An okay card-battler but I found it rather laborious. Battles have a habit of going on forever but lack the mechanical variety required to keep the excitement running over long card exchanges.\n\nTo its credit, the battle system is deceptively complex - at first it seems very simple and one dimensional but it does expand in depth quite quickly with the introduction of about half a dozen different \"blocks\" that can be used during fights to your advantage (or disadvantage).\n\nWhen weighing up the time I enjoyed with the game versus the time I spent wishing things would just move along, I ultimately feel it's more of a thumbs down than a thumbs up for me. It's a casual card battler and that's fine - the complexity isn't something I have an issue with.\n\nIt's more that by the time I actually finish a battle, I feel more a sense of relief as opposed to satisfaction or accomplishment. Everything is too \"slow\", which I feel is out of tandem with the casual card battling market the game is trying to appeal to. Check out my full explanation of the game's ups and downs here - https://youtu.be/znfWP-RCadE",
                        "timestamp_created": 1531918371,
                        "timestamp_updated": 1531918522,
                        "voted_up": false,
                        "votes_up": 15,
                        "votes_funny": 0,
                        "weighted_vote_score": 0.6254870891571045,
                        "comment_count": 0,
                        "steam_purchase": true,
                        "received_for_free": false,
                        "written_during_early_access": false,
                        "developer_response": "Hi MaltedBampot,\n\nThanks for your review of Insane Robots.\n\nI wanted to reassure you that AI opponents get tokens with exactly the same probability as you (unless that probability is modified with an augment), including Damage tokens. AIs also have no unfair advantages - they fight with the same knowledge as human players. Actually, the tokens are not as random as they might seem - everyone gets the same tokens and the same fuel, just in random order and random combination. We call this a Tetris deck (based on how probability works in that game).\n\nDamage tokens were introduced to make battles finite, prevent resource \"farming\" in battles and, when used right, maximise overkill. But also they are a \"dirty\" way to end a battle - you get no overkill bonus that way. \n\nFinally, you said that the same song is played constantly throughout the game, but this is not the case. The game actually has a soundtrack of more than 50 tracks, and they were all professionally edited for inclusion in the game. (The title track intentionally doesn't loop, because of its live intro and outro, though it does remain on beat when restarting.)\n\nThanks,\n\nRob",
                        "timestamp_dev_responded": 1533823567,
                        "primarily_steam_deck": false,
                        "refunded": false
                    }
                ],
